    "topic_code": "1.12",
    "topic_title": "Confirming Continuity over an Interval",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = √(x+2)/(x−1). What is the largest interval containing 0 on which f is continuous? Use one-sided continuity at any included endpoint.",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "(−∞, 1)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Ignoring the square-root domain: this interval includes x < −2, where √(x+2) is not real. A function cannot be continuous at points where it is undefined. Fix: Require x+2 ≥ 0 before selecting the interval."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "[−2, 1)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The square root requires x ≥ −2, and the denominator requires x ≠ 1. The quotient is continuous wherever both requirements hold, including right-hand continuity at −2, so the largest interval containing 0 is [−2, 1)."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "[−2, 1]",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Including an undefined endpoint: x = 1 makes the denominator zero, so f(1) is undefined. One-sided continuity does not allow an undefined endpoint to be included. Fix: Check that the function is defined at every included endpoint."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "(−2, 1)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Automatically excluding a domain endpoint: f is continuous on this interval, but it is not the largest possible interval. Since f(−2) = 0 and lim x→−2⁺ f(x) = 0, the endpoint −2 can be included. Fix: Compare the endpoint value with the limit from inside the interval."
     }
    ]
   },
